body:
### What are you trying to do

I design and manufacture saxophone mouthpieces in Rhino. Their internal chambers contain smooth freeform surfaces and undercuts that must be machined with simultaneous 5-axis movements and lollipop cutters.



### How you imagine it working

CamCraft looks very interesting for this application. However, as far as I understand the current implementation, STEP files are tessellated during import and the subsequent toolpath calculations operate on the resulting triangle mesh.

Would it be possible in the future to preserve the original STEP B-rep and NURBS surface information alongside the tessellated display mesh?

For this kind of part, a surface-based workflow would offer several important advantages:

- tool contact points could be calculated on the original surfaces;
- tool-axis directions could use exact or continuously evaluated surface normals;
- smooth 5-axis motion would not depend on triangle density or facet boundaries;
- STEP face boundaries and topology could be retained for selecting machining areas;
- the mesh could still be used for display, stock simulation and collision checking.

I am not suggesting that every existing operation must immediately be converted to an exact B-rep workflow. A useful first step could be to retain the STEP surfaces after import and make them available to selected 3D and 5-axis finishing operations, while keeping the existing mesh-based workflow for STL and OBJ files.

My practical use case would be:

- Rhino STEP model of a saxophone mouthpiece;
- simultaneous 5-axis finishing of the internal chamber;
- ball-nose and lollipop cutters with reduced necks;
- smooth tool-axis changes derived from the original freeform surfaces;
- machining tolerances in approximately the 0.01 mm range.

This would complement the planned form/undercut cutter work in issue #896. I have a real 5-axis machine running Mach4 and could provide a representative STEP model, actual cutter dimensions and practical test feedback if that would be useful.

Thank you for considering it.

Part of #1509.

The first slice of #1509 adds only the Method choice to 2D Adaptive. Under Method = Trochoidal the planner's other numbers are fixed (`crates/motion/src/machining2d/adaptive_troch.rs`, `cut_lane`):

- engagement limit: 90 degrees of contact, measured (the Trochoidal slot's default);
- forward step: at most the optimal load x D (the measured ladder shortens it);
- return between loops: a 0.3 mm microlift, or retract hops when the clearance plane is less than 0.3 mm above the top;
- return feed: the cutting feed;
- loop band: all the room along the medial line;
- the Entry setting is not read (the lane bores its own helix; a note says so when Entry is not Helix).

The stand-alone Trochoidal slot exposes all of these (#220). Wanted: the same numbers on the 2D Adaptive page when Method = Trochoidal (shown only then), in the project file with defaults that keep today's path, and through `edit_operation` over MCP.

Why not derived from the optimal load: a front arc into a full-width band wraps more of the cutter than a straight wall at the same radial bite, so the straight-wall angle of the default load (45 degrees) is never met on a loop (86 degrees measured on the 34 x 16 pin at the default load).

Done means: the fields are on the page under Trochoidal only (structural UI test), round-trip the project file with older files defaulting, are undoable `EditOperationParams` edits and MCP parameters, and a limit no step can hold is refused by name with the measured number.

Part of #1509.

The maintainer on #1509 (2026-11-13): "The trochoidal is a roughing strategy that should be selectable as a way of clearing a pocket, slot or other bulk of material. Anywhere where we can use Adaptive, the option to "tick" trochoidal should also exist."

What the first slice of #1509 does: the Trochoidal method of 2D Adaptive cuts ONE lane of loops along the region's medial line (`crates/motion/src/machining2d/adaptive_troch.rs`), with the loop band the least clearance along that line. It is confined to the region and judged by the same laws as every operation. What it cannot do yet is refused by name or said in the notes:

- a region with no single medial line (branches: L, T, a pocket with an island) is refused: "the area is no single channel ... use the Spiral method for this region";
- a ring round an island (a closed medial line) is refused: "the region closes round an island";
- a region where one lane would leave more than 25 % out of reach is refused (a triangle, a wide area with a narrow neck);
- under 25 % the unreached area (the corners of a rectangular pocket: 9 % on the 34 x 16 pin) is only said in the notes, in mm2 and per cent - nothing cuts it.

Wanted: the Trochoidal method clears any region the Spiral method clears.

Open design (why this is effort:max): several lanes over a wide region (direction, spacing and lane-to-lane engagement, each lane a band inside the eroded area so confinement stays by construction and by the path check), closed lanes round islands (the loop layout's crossing law on a closed centre line), branches, and a wall / corner pass for what the loops cannot reach, held to the engagement limit. The in-planner trochoid of the HSM plan (`adaptive::troch_plan`, #5) is still blocked on #47 (returns land in cut); this issue does not depend on it.

Done means: an L-shaped pocket, a pocket with an island and a wide rectangular pocket each generate under Method = Trochoidal, stay inside the region (the path check and the post's stock sim), read within the engagement limit, and leave no more than the HSM plan's rest share; golden CL for each; the refusals that remain are by name.
